diagnosis_text:
The NOC's claim that a core backbone router R6 or R7 "crashed and is down" is not supported by any current evidence. Direct live inspection of both devices confirms ospfd is running on each, their OSPF adjacencies are Full (R6 holds 3 Full neighbors in area 0.0.0.1; R7 holds 1 Full neighbor in the same area), and both carry 57–59 OSPF routes — entirely normal for this topology. End-to-end reachability between pc0 (20.1.1.10) and pc12 (120.1.1.10) is confirmed at 0% loss in both directions.

All ospf_snapshot flags that initially appeared suspicious proved to be expected design behaviour: r13 and r12 are EIGRP (not OSPF) routers and carry no ospfd by design; r11 is the OSPF↔EIGRP redistribution boundary running both ospfd and eigrpd; r8 is a legitimate multi-area ABR whose area 0.0.0.2 is a stub area (sole router) reached via a correctly functioning OSPF virtual link through r4 (Router-ID 14.1.4.1, VLINK0 Full/adj=1). No ACL drops, no duplicate MACs, no tc shaping, no resource pressure, and no daemon crashes were found on any of the 15 devices.

Root cause: There is no fault. The NOC alert was a false positive — possibly triggered by a transient monitoring hiccup. The OSPF backbone, the EIGRP edge domain, and inter-domain redistribution are all operating normally. No corrective action is required.
